WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.923
When code can’t decide, and a person can’t scale.

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:08.451
Last lesson pushed every criterion as far toward deterministic code as it would go.

3
00:00:08.731 --> 00:00:10.736
Some criteria don’t go far.

4
00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:14.259
Does this reply actually answer the customer’s question?

5
00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:16.907
Is this summary faithful to the transcript?

6
00:00:17.187 --> 00:00:20.109
Does this quote show the named person committing to the task?

7
00:00:20.389 --> 00:00:26.469
These need judgment, and a person reviewing thousands of outputs per release is slow and expensive.

8
00:00:26.749 --> 00:00:36.733
An LLM judge (or LLM-as-judge) is a model call that reads an output, plus whatever context the criterion needs, and returns a verdict.

9
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:47.680
Anthropic’s eval guidance describes it as fast, flexible, scalable, and suitable for complex judgment, with one condition: test that it’s reliable first, then scale.

10
00:00:47.960 --> 00:01:00.077
Anthropic’s engineering write-up on agent evals lists the weaknesses on the same page: judges are non-deterministic, cost more than code, and need calibration against human graders.

11
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:04.667
So a judge is a component you build and measure, not an oracle you consult.

12
00:01:04.947 --> 00:01:20.797
Use one when three things are true: the criterion matters enough to track; no code check (or decomposition into code checks) captures it; and you can get a person who knows the domain to label examples, so you can find out whether the judge agrees with them.

13
00:01:21.077 --> 00:01:22.805
That last step is lesson 6.

14
00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:26.264
This lesson is about building a judge worth calibrating.

15
00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:33.925
A good default from Studio 1 carries over: ask a narrow question about one criterion, for one output at a time.

16
00:01:34.205 --> 00:01:37.704
“Review this conversation” invites a vague, lenient answer.

17
00:01:37.984 --> 00:01:42.080
“Does the reply make any policy claim the policy excerpt doesn’t support?

18
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.736
Pass or fail, with the reason” is something a model, and a person, can answer consistently.
